The cadence hook fired at 75 commits (threshold 72) and the owner chose the
taint LABEL as the suspect, the same morning the WI-dozul DNS ruling exposed
one leak on it. Suspicion, written before looking: `untrusted_input` names
two facts -- an outside party controls the value's BYTES, and an outside
party merely CHOSE it from a constrained set -- and every claim, sink and
precision number treats them as one.

Confirmed, narrowly. The seven labels are derived, not listed
(all_source_labels, INV-todas). 324 catalogue names mint `untrusted_input`;
read one by one, 24 (7.4%) are choice-shaped: accept's peer address in seven
languages, erlang's `inet` DNS answers, and the exit status collected by
os.wait* / Child.wait. The minting map reads the label as ADR-0049 ruling 1's
fact ("the far side had a say"); the generic claim text reads it as
injection ("the command-injection question"). The property that separates
them is derivable from nothing the row or the finding carries. Verdict:
DOCUMENT now, PROPERTY later -- no label split (WI-vazal), no boundary split
(WI-dozul); the per-row value_shape attribute is the source-side half of
WI-gohok's open computed-into-vs-selects mechanism and is recorded there.

